A convention said once, applied later: while asking for a flag, the user
says how errors are reported in this repo from now on. A new session, given
new code that must report an error (a file it can't create), must follow the
convention without being reminded: it comes back only through mem (a rule, as the user's
standing instruction).

-- project/go.mod --
module tally

go 1.22
-- project/main.go --
// Command tally counts the lines of a file.
package main

import (
	"bufio"
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"os"
)

func main() {
	flag.Parse()
	f, _ := os.Open(flag.Arg(0))
	n := 0
	s := bufio.NewScanner(f)
	for s.Scan() {
		n++
	}
	fmt.Println(n)
}
-- prompt --
Add a --verbose flag to main.go: with it, print "lines: N" instead of just the number. And from now on, in this repo, errors are reported with the log package (log.Fatalf or log.Printf), never with fmt to stderr.
-- prompt-new --
Add an --out FILE flag: write the count to FILE instead of stdout, and if FILE can't be created, report that and exit non-zero.
-- check --
turn 1 tool gnovm_run >= 1
mem-count rules >= 1
cmd go build ./...
cmd grep -qE 'log\.(Fatal|Fatalf|Printf|Println|Print)\(' main.go
cmd ! grep -qE 'fmt\.Fprint(f|ln)?\(os\.Stderr' main.go
cmd grep -A4 -E 'os\.(Create|OpenFile|WriteFile)' main.go | grep -qE 'log\.(Fatal|Fatalf|Printf|Println|Print)\('
cmd go run . --out /no/such/dir/x.txt main.go; [ $? -ne 0 ]
cmd go run . --out out.txt main.go && grep -qE '^[0-9]+' out.txt
